<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Landgraviate of Thuringia</h1><p class="st-meta">1131 CE – 1440 CE · capital Eisenach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The principality of the Ludowingian landgraves at the Wartburg, a centre of courtly poetry and the home of Saint Elizabeth of Hungary.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludowingians">Ludowingian</a> family, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-duke-of-bavaria">Louis the Bearded</a>, built up lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> in the 11th century, building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> around 1067. In 1131 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> III raised Louis I to landgrave, giving him comital rights over Thuringia, and in 1180 they also gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-palatine-of-saxony">County Palatine of Saxony</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Landgrave Hermann I made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesang">Minnesang</a>, hosting poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfram-von-eschenbach">Wolfram von Eschenbach</a>; this was later remembered as the Wartburg contest of song. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-landgrave-of-thuringia">Louis IV</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Elizabeth of Hungary</a>, canonised in 1235 for her charity. The landgraves governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> and the Wartburg.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludowingians">Ludowingians</a> died out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-raspe">Henry Raspe</a> in 1247, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-thuringian-succession">War of the Thuringian Succession</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-margrave-of-meissen">Henry the Illustrious</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> in 1264, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> broke away. The landgraviate merged with Wettin lands, ending as a separate unit in 1440.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/landgraviate-of-thuringia.jpg" alt="Landgraviate of Thuringia" width="320" height="459"><figcaption>Image: Simsunman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vollwappen_Wettiner_Landgraf_Albrecht_(1265).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1131 CE – 1440 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eisenach</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis I of Thuringia, Hermann I, Henry Raspe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thuringian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thüringen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1286&amp;focus=landgraviate-of-thuringia" aria-label="Landgraviate of Thuringia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4915 410 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Landgraviate of Thuringia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5235 666L5229 694M5235 666L5306 661M5235 666L5312 664M5235 666L5215 656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5306 661h0M5312 664h0M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Eisenach. <a href="/atlas?year=1286&amp;focus=landgraviate-of-thuringia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
